 formality pairwise is marked as P0 but blocked by exp-001's completion, so under median tier constraints it probably won't finish or will only be partial. Exp-003 comparing Likert versus pairwise sensitivity across judges similarly depends on those earlier experiments and will have limited data. Exp-005 on pairwise RATE control is independent and would run on GPT-4o with LLMBar but skip the full judge-by-benchmark expansion. Exp-006 on scaled replication depends on exp-001 and exp-002, making it unlikely to happen or only partially complete. For exp-008 on register axis scoring, verification passed at 94% NLI accuracy but API costs are blocking the scoring step—under median tier, Qwen3-32B scoring is done but the work remains incomplete.\n\nExp-009 on non-Qwen rewriter control is P2 and probably won't get done. The key finding for median tier is that exp-020 shows GPT-4o verbosity Likert ATE is non-significant, completing all 6 Blindspot cases, while register scoring shows p=0.085 which is not significant. I'm realizing the overlap here—exp-008's remaining work on GPT-4o register scoring is actually part of what exp-020 covers, so I need to clarify how these experiments map together. Looking at the median tier scenario, most of the planned experiments won't fully materialize. exp-001 would only partially complete with a single judge on formality, exp-002 requires new costly CF generation so it's off the table, and exp-003 ends up with incomplete data. exp-005 runs just on GPT-4o without the extended scope, exp-006 depends on the earlier experiments so it doesn't happen, but exp-008 and exp-020 do complete with the GPT-4o verbosity and register scoring work. exp-009 gets deprioritized and doesn't run.\n\nNow I'm working through the specific outcome numbers for each experiment. The remaining experiments hit resource constraints—exp-002 stalled on counterfactual generation for AlpacaEval due to API and compute limits, exp-003 only partially completed with 2-3 judges on formality showing the same pattern (Likert non-significant, pairwise significant), and exp-005 ran just on GPT-4o with LLMBar, revealing that double-rewriting introduces a weak original preference effect but it's much smaller than the formality effect.\n\nExp-006 couldn't run because it depends on the incomplete exp-001/002, so budget went toward exp-020 instead to complete the 2×3 matrix. For exp-008/exp-020, I'm scoring GPT-4o on verbosity and register—verbosity pairwise is already highly significant at 86.3%, but the Likert ATE is non-significant, which confirms the blindspot pattern across all six conditions, while register pairwise remains at 63.3% (not significant) and stays exploratory.\n\nExp-009 didn't complete because it was lower priority and the budget ran out on higher-priority experiments.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the YAML structure should look like and what tables and figures would actually be generated from these results. I need to be more precise about the metrics and rationales rather than just marking things as incomplete. Looking back at the tier definition, the median scenario means some experiments genuinely don't finish—that's the predicted outcome itself. Let me reconsider how to structure this properly, especially around the multi-judge partial completion where exactly one additional judge gets tested on 1-2 axes, which maps to exp-001 partially running.\n\nFor the verbosity experiments, the Likert ATE is still pending but the median tier predicts non-significance, which would confirm a blindspot across both judges and all three axes. I'm also reviewing the register results more carefully—the pairwise result for GPT-4o register shows non-significance at p=0.085, so that's not a clear blindspot pattern like the others. Looking at the evidence matrix, I see that GPT-4o verbosity is the only cell with a pending result—once I confirm whether that Likert ATE comes back as non-significant, I'll have 5 out of 6 cells showing the blindspot pattern where pairwise detects significance but Likert doesn't. The register cells are messier since the pairwise results themselves are marginal or non-significant, so they don't fit the clean blindspot definition. So the register cells show a pattern where Likert consistently fails to detect bias across both models, but GEE reveals significant style effects when controlling for originality—that's the core of the Blindspot definition. Rather than getting caught up in the nuances, I should move forward with generating the YAML predictions the user is asking for.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the three planned experiments to their median-tier results. For the multi-judge formality experiment, I have partial data from Claude-3.5-Sonnet showing a strong pairwise effect around 0.62 but no Likert signal, while Gemini's data remains incomplete due to budget constraints. The multi-benchmark experiment is blocked entirely pending completion of the first experiment and new counterfactual generation. For the sensitivity comparison across judges, I only have partial results from three judges on the formality dimension so far.\n\nLooking at the remaining experiments, there's a consistent blindspot pattern showing up in 5 or 6 out of 6 available data points. The pairwise rate control experiment ran only on GPT-4o with LLMBar, finding a marginally significant preference around 57% that wasn't extended further. The scaled replication is still blocked on earlier experiments, while register scoring is queued—GPT-4o's version will complete as part of a larger experiment, and Qwen3-32B is already done. The non-Qwen rewriter control wasn't executed due to budget exhaustion. Finally, for the GPT-4o verbosity experiment, the Likert effect is null as expected, though the pairwise preference is already at 86.3%.\n\nNow I'm compiling the metric ranges and thresholds for the YAML configuration, setting up the evidence matrix with specific bounds for win probabilities, blindspot confirmation rates, and various effect size measures. Writing YAML config...\n\nThe key constraint is that only Claude completed the full formality scoring across 200 pairs with statistical significance at p=0.005, while Gemini ran out of API budget partway through, so I'm working with incomplete data from just one additional judge. The expected results show Claude maintaining a 62% win probability with confidence intervals between 54-70%, which should help stabilize the review score if the writing quality holds up. The third judge confirms the directional consistency with GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B on formality bias, though the effect size is moderate. The Likert scale analysis remains non-significant with a high tie rate, and the blindspot pattern holds across judges. Gemini couldn't be fully analyzed due to budget constraints, but the partial data suggests alignment with the same trend. This multi-judge expansion deepens the findings on one axis rather than broadening across multiple dimensions, which supports a scope-qualified claim but falls short of demonstrating generalizability. Generating markdown table...\n\nI've added the Claude-3.5-Sonnet formality data point, and all three judges' confidence intervals exclude 0.50 with consistent direction. This confirms the formality bias is consistent across models on this single dimension, though it's more of a finding-level contribution than a generalizability claim. The incomplete Gemini results are typical when ambition outpaces available resources.\n\nThe planned multi-benchmark formality comparison across AlpacaEval and MT-Bench never started because the counterfactual generation wasn't initiated. Experiment 001 only completed partially with one judge, which was a prerequisite for this work. The API budget got consumed by experiments 001 and 020, leaving insufficient funds to proceed with the new counterfactual generation.\n\nGPT-4o 的冗长度和语域评分消耗了剩余预算，而多基准测试需要新源文本的反事实生成和三个评判者在两个基准上的大量调用，总成本远超可用额度。这直接限制了论文的范围，只能在 LLMBar 基准上验证，基准泛化性未能测试，虽然审稿人可能会指出这一点，但根据论文标准这不会成为拒稿理由。\n\n我需要在局限性部分明确说明结果仅在单一基准上演示，跨基准泛化留作未来工作。\n\n对于 Likert 与配对比较的敏感性分析，我基于 exp-001 的部分完成数据，对三个评判者在形式性维度上进行系统比较，但冗长度和语域仅有两个评判者的数据。\n\n预期结果显示形式性维度三个评判者都存在盲点，配对比较相比 Likert 的敏感性提升超过 10 倍，中位数档的盲点确认率在 0.67 到 0.83 之间。\n\n虽然跨两个主要评判者有 6 个单元格中的 5 个得到确认，但考虑到评判者覆盖的不均匀性，保守报告为\"2 个主要评判者中 5/6 单元格确认，加上第三个评判者的方向性支持\"。Likert 并列率在评判者间保持稳定，范围在 0.60 到 0.73 之间。\n\n现在我在查看一个汇总表，对比 Likert 和配对比较的检测能力，显示了各个评判者在不同维度上的 p 值、并列率和盲点确认情况。\n\n我还在整理一个热力图来可视化这些 p 值的矩阵对比，用颜色标记出盲点的分布模式。\n\n不过 C2 Likert 盲点虽然在现有数据上表现一致，但受限于评判者覆盖的不均匀性，所以论文需要明确区分哪些是两个评判者都完整测试过的情况，哪些只有一个评判者部分测试过，这样才能避免过度声称跨评判者的泛化能力。\n\n接下来还有一个针对 GPT-4o 的配对比较实验，用来量化原始文本偏好的基线水平，通过对 100 个正式性配对的对比来控制这个混淆因素，虽然这个实验目前只限于这一个评判者和基准的组合。\n\n根据预期结果，原始文本的胜率大约是 57%，置信区间在 49% 到 65% 之间，p 值约为 0.12，这表明虽然存在原始文本偏好但并不显著。这与之前的 GEE 分析结果一致，说明原始文本偏好确实存在但弱于风格效应的影响。之所以在中位数档没有扩展到多个评判者，主要是因为预算优先用于完成其他更关键的实验。 看这个表格的补充数据，RATE 控制验证了风格效应不能完全用原始偏好来解释，而 GEE 分解提供了更有力的证据，同时建模两个效应。现有的发现已经足够充分，不需要扩展到所有组合。由于前面的实验还未完全完成，扩展复制计划暂时搁置，API 预算优先用于完成核心的 2×3 证据矩阵。\n\n当前 n=100 的置信区间宽度约 15 个百分点，已经排除了 0.50，统计结论已经很稳健，这个精度水平对于现有发现级别来说已经足够了。\n\n虽然 n=300-500 可能对 power curve 论证有帮助，但这不是决定性因素——我打算通过 power curve 图表来论证当前样本量的充分性，而不是盲目追求更大的 n。\n\n不执行这个扩展复制不会损害论文的 Findings 评分，因为 power curve 图表已经表明 n=100 对于两两比较是足够的，而 n≈462 才是二值化李克特量表所需的，这反过来支持了 C2 盲点中关于 power 不足的论证。\n\n现在看到下一个实验计划涉及 GPT-4o 对 94 个 NLI 验证样本的评分。 I'm seeing the additional metrics for the originality dimension showing a much stronger effect with GPT-4o (OR=3.50, p=0.002), which aligns with the Qwen3-32B pattern where originality dominates. The academic style effect remains weak and marginally significant across both judges, suggesting a consistent pattern in how they evaluate the register dimension. OR 比 Qwen 更弱是合理的——GPT-4o 整体 style sensitivity 低于 Qwen（formality: 63% vs 66.5%），register 作为最弱 axis 在 GPT-4o 上可能不显著。这支持 register 作为 \"method boundary case study\" 的 median 档叙事——axes where content change dominates style change are harder to audit。\n\n现在看 register 在两个 judge 上的交叉对比，Qwen3-32B 的 originality effect 更强（OR 5.72 vs 3.5